--- abstract

Participation in the IETF is by individuals.  This document applies
that principle now that software agents can write and submit text.
Each identity belongs to one human, and every Contribution has at least
one accountable human.  Anonymous Contributions are not accepted, and
no human may use two identities in the same piece of work.  Reading the
IETF's work requires no account or identification.  Rough consensus is
unchanged.

--- middle

# Introduction

The IETF has always said that its participants act as individuals
({{existing-principle}}).  Those statements assumed that a person typed
every message and every draft.  Agents can now draft text, post to
mailing lists, open issues, and submit documents, at a volume no person
could.

This document doesn't restrict the tools people use.  It says who is
responsible for what they produce.

## Out of Scope {#out-of-scope}

This document covers the link between a human and their identities.
It does not cover coordination between separate humans, such as
"meatpuppets" recruited or directed to support a position, or the
relationships between humans and their employers, sponsors, or other
affiliations.

Rough consensus is not determined by counting participants
{{RFC7282}}.  Chairs and the IESG must still apply judgement, including
to participation that appears coordinated.  This document changes
neither.

# Conventions and Definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

This document uses "Contribution", "Contributor", and "Participant" as
defined in {{RFC8179}} and {{RFC5378}}.

Human:
: A natural person.

Identity:
: The name a human participates under, with the email addresses and
  accounts they use, such as a Datatracker account, a mailing list
  subscription, or a code repository account.

Agent:
: Software that acts on behalf of a human, including by producing or
  submitting text.

# Requirements

No Datatracker account is required.  Taking part only by email remains
sufficient.

## One Identity per Human

Each human SHOULD use one identity; {{multiple-identities}} covers the
exception.  An identity MAY include several email addresses and
accounts, for example for different affiliations or for personal and
work use.  Together they are one participant.

## One Human per Identity {#one-human-per-identity}

Each identity MUST belong to exactly one human, who is bound by the
Note Well {{NOTEWELL}} like every participant.  An identity MUST NOT be
shared.

An organization or role, such as a working group's chairs or the IETF
Secretariat, may act through its own address or account.  It MUST have
one or more accountable humans, who need not be publicly named.  Each
is accountable as if they had acted under their own identity.  Such an
address is not an identity and is not counted as a participant.

## Every Contribution Has an Accountable Human

Every Contribution MUST be vouched for by at least one human, who is
accountable for it as a Contributor.  They MAY use any tools, including
agents.  Contributor obligations, including IPR disclosure {{RFC8179}}
and conduct {{RFC7154}}, apply whether the human typed the text or an
agent produced it.

Nobody has to say which tools they used, though it is polite to,
except where the IPR rules {{RFC5378}} {{RFC8179}} require disclosure.

## Co-authored Contributions

When a Contribution has several authors, such as an Internet-Draft or
a joint review, each author is a Contributor and fully accountable for
it.  Obligations are not divided between them.

Each author MUST be a separate human.  An agent MUST NOT be listed as
an author, and a human MUST NOT list another of their own identities
as a co-author.

# Anonymity {#anonymity}

This document uses "anonymous" and "pseudonym" as defined in
{{Section 3.3 of RFC6973}}.

Anonymity has real value: it lets people take part in political life
when speaking under their own name would endanger them, their
families, or their jobs {{RFC8280}}.  It is still incompatible with the
IETF standards process, which depends on knowing who made each
Contribution:

* Contributors grant rights and make representations
  ({{Section 5 of RFC5378}}).  From nobody in particular, these are
  worth nothing.

* IPR disclosure covers IPR the Contributor "reasonably and personally"
  knows of, including their employer's or sponsor's
  ({{Section 5.6 of RFC8179}}).  Nobody can check that without knowing
  who the Contributor is.

* A human who takes part in the same discussion under several
  undisclosed identities is deliberately misleading the chairs and
  other participants.  There are other ways to mislead; this document
  addresses only this one.

* Conduct guidelines {{RFC7154}} and moderation {{RFC9945}} act on
  people.  A remedy escaped by creating a new identity has no effect.

* Affiliations "are often used for identification"
  ({{Section 3.2 of RFC9281}}).  Readers weigh an argument partly by
  who is making it.

Every Contribution MUST therefore be attributable to an identified
human.  The IETF does not accept anonymous Contributions.

## Reading Requires No Identity

These requirements apply to contributing, not reading.  The IETF
commits to making its documents, mailing lists, attendance lists, and
minutes publicly available ({{Section 1 of RFC3935}}), and keeps a
"publicly accessible record" ({{Section 8 of RFC2026}}).

Anyone MUST be able to read Internet-Drafts, RFCs, mailing list
archives, issue trackers, meeting materials, minutes, and recordings
without an account, a login, or identifying themselves.  A reader can
stay anonymous.  A contributor can't.

Meetings already draw this line.  Attendance has long been recorded,
first on paper blue sheets and now by logging in to Meetecho
({{RFC9592}}), and attendance lists are public.  Minutes, materials,
and recordings are open to anyone.  This document applies the same
split everywhere.

# Multiple Identities {#multiple-identities}

A human MAY use more than one identity without publicly linking them,
for example to work in an area that is sensitive for their employer or
government, or to separate personal and professional work.  Each
identity is subject to every requirement in this document.

A human MUST NOT use more than one identity in the same piece of work,
which includes at least:

* an Internet-Draft, any document derived from it, and any discussion,
  issue, review, or consensus call about them;

* a working group charter or BoF, and any discussion about either; and

* any nomination, petition, or other process in which participants
  are counted.

A human MUST NOT create a new identity to escape a remedy, or their
history on an existing topic.

# Enforcement {#enforcement}

Breaches are handled through the moderation processes in {{RFC9945}}.
Remedies apply to the human, and so to all of their identities.

# Open Questions

This document is a starting point.  These questions are open:

* Should a "piece of work" be a single document or a whole working
  group?  Working group scope is easier to check, but constrains people
  with good reasons to keep lines of work apart.

* Should unlinked identities be linked privately, for example to the
  IETF Secretariat or the Ombudsteam, so that {{multiple-identities}}
  can be enforced without public exposure?

* Is a pseudonymous identity acceptable if the human behind it is known
  to the IETF and equally accountable?

* Without a Datatracker account, what ties an email address to an
  identity?

* How should the IETF verify that an identity belongs to one human,
  and how much verification is proportionate?

# Existing Principle {#existing-principle}

The following documents state that participation in the IETF is by
individuals.

{{Section 3.2 of RFC9281}} (BCP 11):

> For all purposes relevant to the Internet Standards development
> process, membership in the IETF and its WGs is defined to be
> established solely and entirely by individuals who participate in
> IETF and WG activities.  These individuals do not formally represent
> any organizations they may be affiliated with, although affiliations
> are often used for identification.

{{Section 1 of RFC2418}} (BCP 25):

> There is no formal membership in the IETF.  Participation is open to
> all. \[...\] Participation is by individual technical contributors,
> rather than by formal representatives of organizations.

{{Section 5 of RFC8179}} (BCP 79):

> This document refers to the IETF Participant making disclosures,
> consistent with the general IETF philosophy that Participants in the
> IETF act as individuals.

{{Section 1 of RFC8179}} defines a "Contributor" as "an individual
submitting a Contribution", and a "Participant" as "any individual
Participating in an IETF discussion or activity".  {{Section 1 of
RFC5378}} (BCP 78) uses the same definition of "Contributor".

{{Section 2.2 of RFC7241}}:

> All IETF participants act as individuals.  There is no concept of
> "IETF membership".

{{Section 4.1 of RFC9680}}:

> While IETF participants are expected to participate as individuals,
> their actions could still be construed as representing their
> employer, whatever their role.

{{Section 2.1 of RFC8718}}:

> Meeting attendees participate as individuals.

{{Appendix A.1 of RFC6702}} describes the IPR obligations as "personal
requirements applying to all IETF participants as individuals".

{{Section 5 of RFC2026}} notes that the IAB and IESG "are composed of
individuals who may participate, as individuals, in the technical work
of the IETF".

The final version of the Tao of the IETF, archived in {{RFC9592}}, says
"There is no membership in the IETF."

The IRTF's code of conduct {{RFC9775}} says a generative tool must not
be listed as an author of a document, and that significant amounts of
AI-generated content must be disclosed.

# Security Considerations

Denial of service:
: Agents can produce Contributions faster than people can read them.
  This document doesn't limit volume, but every Contribution has an
  accountable human that moderation {{RFC9945}} can act on, and all of
  a human's identities count as one.

False identification:
: A claim that two identities are the same person can be used to
  discredit someone or discount their support.  Such a finding needs
  evidence and a chance to respond.  If it is wrong, it and any remedy
  based on it must be withdrawn.

Credential compromise:
: Contributions made by someone who has taken over a human's address
  or account were not vouched for by that human, who should report the
  compromise promptly.  A claim of compromise can also be false, made to
  disown a Contribution, and is judged on its evidence.  Credentials
  given to an agent are not compromised: the human is accountable for
  what the agent does.

Privacy:
: A link between identities, or between an identity and an
  affiliation, can expose a human to the risks in {{anonymity}}.  Any
  private record of such links is sensitive, and access to it should be
  limited to those enforcing this document.

